WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.466
Welcome to Module 5 of the
Privileged Access learning path:

2
00:00:04.466 --> 00:00:06.900
PIM for Groups, and the P R V groups.

3
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:11.676
This module serves the project
plan's task A2.1 in Phase 2,

4
00:00:11.676 --> 00:00:16.763
and A3.3 in Phase 3, with Chapter
05 of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:16.763 --> 00:00:20.187
The program never assigns a
privileged role to a person.

6
00:00:20.187 --> 00:00:25.375
It assigns the role to a role-assignable
group named P R V, then the tier,

7
00:00:25.375 --> 00:00:30.563
then the role, so the tier shows in
every review, alert and audit record.

8
00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:32.662
Module 1 reads those groups.

9
00:00:32.662 --> 00:00:34.101
This module writes them.

10
00:00:34.101 --> 00:00:39.479
You keep each group's own state in one
reviewed file: who is a member or owner,

11
00:00:39.479 --> 00:00:43.800
until when, on which ticket,
and how membership is activated.

12
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:49.035
The code reads the tenant first, reports
what's wrong, plans the exact requests,

13
00:00:49.035 --> 00:00:52.962
and applies them only when you
ask, and only past its guards.

14
00:00:52.962 --> 00:00:57.180
It's illustrative: tested
against fixtures and a fake.

15
00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.080
Why does this matter?

16
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.249
Because the P R V groups
decide who holds each role,

17
00:01:05.249 --> 00:01:07.955
and their state drifts like anything else.

18
00:01:07.955 --> 00:01:11.019
So the guards refuse a plan
before anything is sent,

19
00:01:11.019 --> 00:01:14.593
and three of them keep people
from losing access by mistake.

20
00:01:14.593 --> 00:01:18.668
Guard G four refuses eligible
owners with no active owner.

21
00:01:18.668 --> 00:01:22.610
Microsoft Learn's page on PIM
for Groups says the last active

22
00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.868
owner can't be removed, so an
activated owner's deactivation fails.

23
00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:31.162
Guard G six refuses more removals
than the Max Removals limit,

24
00:01:31.162 --> 00:01:35.793
five by default, because that's the
sign of a wrong or truncated file.

25
00:01:35.793 --> 00:01:42.181
And guard G one refuses a Tier 0 or Tier
1 policy losing approval or its approvers.

26
00:01:42.181 --> 00:01:44.819
Microsoft Learn also says a group can't be

27
00:01:44.819 --> 00:01:47.380
taken back out of PIM once it's onboarded.

28
00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.330
So Lab 2 uses groups made for the lab.

29
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.920
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

30
00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.567
which takes about fifty-five
minutes with the lab.

31
00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:03.857
You'll explain the two Microsoft
features that meet in a P R V group,

32
00:02:03.857 --> 00:02:06.955
and the two ways the
program uses them together.

33
00:02:06.955 --> 00:02:10.250
You'll name what Microsoft
Learn says about role-assignable

34
00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.755
groups and PIM for Groups
that shapes the code.

35
00:02:12.755 --> 00:02:17.430
You'll read the findings, P G oh
one to oh nine, and a plan's diff.

36
00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.053
And you'll name the
guards, G one to G seven,

37
00:02:20.053 --> 00:02:23.630
and say why the pipeline's
app doesn't create the groups.

38
00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:25.661
Before you start, finish Module 4,

39
00:02:25.661 --> 00:02:30.550
for eligible assignments and the P R V
group that holds each role's eligibility,

40
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.333
and read about
role-assignable groups in the

41
00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:36.642
chapter on privileged tiers
in Access and Privilege.

42
00:02:36.642 --> 00:02:41.310
Lab 2 needs a worldwide or G C C
test tenant with Microsoft Entra I

43
00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:46.600
D P2 or Governance licenses and
fictional people, never production.

44
00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:53.525
Unit 1: what the P R
V groups have to hold.

45
00:02:53.525 --> 00:02:56.987
Two Microsoft features
meet in a P R V group.

46
00:02:56.987 --> 00:02:59.263
The first is role-assignable groups.

47
00:02:59.263 --> 00:03:04.026
Microsoft Learn's page on using groups
to manage role assignments says only

48
00:03:04.026 --> 00:03:08.713
a group created with is Assignable To
Role set to true can hold an Entra role.

49
00:03:08.713 --> 00:03:11.313
It's set at creation and can't be changed.

50
00:03:11.313 --> 00:03:13.450
A tenant can have at most five hundred.

51
00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:18.388
Membership must be assigned, never
dynamic, and a group can't be a member.

52
00:03:18.388 --> 00:03:22.733
The second is PIM for Groups:
just-in-time membership or ownership,

53
00:03:22.733 --> 00:03:26.275
with one policy for membership
and one for ownership.

54
00:03:26.275 --> 00:03:29.637
The program's design uses
them together in two ways.

55
00:03:29.637 --> 00:03:33.085
For an Entra role, the group
holds an eligible assignment,

56
00:03:33.085 --> 00:03:34.699
and its members are active.

57
00:03:34.699 --> 00:03:39.350
For other privilege, such as an Azure role,
the members are eligible for the group.

58
00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:45.800
The access packages for P R V membership,
the plan's A3.3, aren't built here.

59
00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:53.800
Three facts from Microsoft Learn's
PIM for Groups pages shape the code.

60
00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:57.927
First, eligible membership doesn't
appear in the group's members list,

61
00:03:57.927 --> 00:04:01.475
so reading members alone would
miss every eligible holder.

62
00:04:01.475 --> 00:04:05.852
Second, PIM onboards a group on its
first schedule or policy change,

63
00:04:05.852 --> 00:04:10.787
and the group can't be taken back out;
the policy I D's change when that happens.

64
00:04:10.787 --> 00:04:15.426
Third, group administrators or
owners can override what PIM set.

65
00:04:15.426 --> 00:04:18.164
So the code reads the
tenant before every plan,

66
00:04:18.164 --> 00:04:20.975
rather than trusting what
it applied last time.

67
00:04:20.975 --> 00:04:22.903
And one choice of the kit's own:

68
00:04:22.903 --> 00:04:27.360
activations aren't standing state,
so the read leaves them out.

69
00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:34.935
Unit 2: the file, the
findings and the guards.

70
00:04:34.935 --> 00:04:38.575
The desired state is one
file, P R V groups dot json,

71
00:04:38.575 --> 00:04:44.398
with the tier policy beside it; the values
are this workbook's design guidance.

72
00:04:44.398 --> 00:04:50.136
Every member and owner policy selects
Module 3's authentication context, c1,

73
00:04:50.136 --> 00:04:56.148
as the Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 04, Access and Privilege, requires.

74
00:04:56.148 --> 00:05:01.451
So, as in Module 4, the Apply switch also
needs the Activation Gate Checked switch,

75
00:05:01.451 --> 00:05:04.585
a person's statement that
the gate's policy is On.

76
00:05:04.585 --> 00:05:09.835
The read compares the tenant with it
and raises rules P G oh one to oh nine.

77
00:05:09.835 --> 00:05:14.660
Oh one is a name that doesn't follow
the P R V pattern, the program's naming.

78
00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:18.670
Oh two is a group that holds an
Entra role but isn't role-assignable,

79
00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:21.418
and oh three a dynamic
or synchronized group;

80
00:05:21.418 --> 00:05:24.760
Microsoft Learn says PIM for
Groups can't manage those.

81
00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:27.502
Oh four counts
role-assignable groups against

82
00:05:27.502 --> 00:05:29.822
Microsoft Learn's limit of five hundred.

83
00:05:29.822 --> 00:05:34.573
Oh five is a group nested as a holder,
or a service principal made eligible.

84
00:05:34.573 --> 00:05:37.885
Oh six is an entry with
no end or no ticket.

85
00:05:37.885 --> 00:05:39.772
Oh seven is the owner trap.

86
00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:43.335
Oh eight is a policy rule that
differs from the reviewed setting.

87
00:05:43.335 --> 00:05:47.195
And oh nine is anything the
file doesn't grant or list:

88
00:05:47.195 --> 00:05:50.610
anything outside the file is unreviewed.

89
00:05:54.360 --> 00:06:00.448
The plan has a fixed order: groups, then
policy rules, then grants, then removals.

90
00:06:00.448 --> 00:06:03.898
And seven guards refuse a
plan before anything is sent.

91
00:06:03.898 --> 00:06:09.448
G one, a Tier 0 or Tier 1 policy
losing approval or its approvers.

92
00:06:09.448 --> 00:06:14.247
G two, a rule that allows permanent
grants, or a grant with no end.

93
00:06:14.247 --> 00:06:17.448
G three, a group set as its own approver.

94
00:06:17.448 --> 00:06:19.422
G four, the owner trap.

95
00:06:19.422 --> 00:06:23.960
G five, a group as a holder, or
an eligible service principal.

96
00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:26.360
G six, too many removals.

97
00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:29.560
And G seven, a self-service
action in the plan.

98
00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:34.172
One more choice: the pipeline's app
doesn't create the P R V groups.

99
00:06:34.172 --> 00:06:37.297
Microsoft Learn's reference
for creating a group says a

100
00:06:37.297 --> 00:06:41.510
role-assignable group needs Role
Management dot ReadWrite dot Directory.

101
00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:44.349
The program keeps that
permission off the pipeline,

102
00:06:44.349 --> 00:06:47.848
and a Privileged Role Administrator
creates the groups by hand.

103
00:06:47.848 --> 00:06:50.530
That's this workbook's design guidance.

104
00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.005
Try it.

105
00:06:55.005 --> 00:07:00.542
Lab 1 runs in one PowerShell 7 session,
in the privileged-access folder.

106
00:07:00.542 --> 00:07:05.892
Step one runs Microsoft's samples
against the fake, as published: ten pass.

107
00:07:05.892 --> 00:07:09.165
Step two sends Microsoft
Learn's Example 1 yourself,

108
00:07:09.165 --> 00:07:13.655
from the reference page for creating
an eligibility schedule request.

109
00:07:13.655 --> 00:07:17.805
You load the module and the sample
harness, and read the first example.

110
00:07:17.805 --> 00:07:22.167
Its title says an admin creates an
eligible group ownership request.

111
00:07:22.167 --> 00:07:24.818
But its body's access I D says member.

112
00:07:24.818 --> 00:07:28.554
So the example is labeled
ownership and asks for membership.

113
00:07:28.554 --> 00:07:31.729
Then send it to the fake:
four hundred, Bad Request,

114
00:07:31.729 --> 00:07:37.205
because the schedule ended in February twenty
twenty-three, before the fake's clock.

115
00:07:37.205 --> 00:07:38.505
Its dates are stale.

116
00:07:38.505 --> 00:07:42.170
Samples are a starting
point, not a desired state.

117
00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:48.907
Step three lists the
permissions the read needs.

118
00:07:48.907 --> 00:07:53.320
Four: Group dot Read Basic dot All,
and the three PIM for Groups reads,

119
00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:58.695
for assignment schedules, eligibility
schedules and role management policies.

120
00:07:58.695 --> 00:08:00.370
Nothing ends in ReadWrite.

121
00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:04.283
Step four plans against the
fictional tenant, as a dry run.

122
00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:06.467
You give it the cloud, the fixture,

123
00:08:06.467 --> 00:08:09.945
the desired state file, the
date and an output folder.

124
00:08:09.945 --> 00:08:13.445
The read raises eleven
findings and three warnings.

125
00:08:13.445 --> 00:08:16.518
The plan has ten changes:
three assignments,

126
00:08:16.518 --> 00:08:19.420
two eligibilities and five policy rules.

127
00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:22.320
The guards all passed, so
the plan could be applied,

128
00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:25.856
but the run warns that it selects
an authentication context,

129
00:08:25.856 --> 00:08:29.533
so the apply will also need the
Activation Gate Checked switch.

130
00:08:29.533 --> 00:08:31.956
And it's a dry run, so nothing was sent:

131
00:08:31.956 --> 00:08:35.990
review the diff and the plan,
then run again with Apply.

132
00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:41.852
Step five prints the findings.

133
00:08:41.852 --> 00:08:45.698
Two warnings are P G oh nine for
groups the file doesn't list:

134
00:08:45.698 --> 00:08:49.703
a legacy help desk group and an
Intune administrators group.

135
00:08:49.703 --> 00:08:53.002
Then P R V T0 Priv Role Admin.

136
00:08:53.002 --> 00:08:56.616
It has a permanent active
member the file doesn't grant,

137
00:08:56.616 --> 00:08:58.302
which is oh six and oh nine.

138
00:08:58.302 --> 00:09:01.463
It has an eligible owner
with no active owner,

139
00:09:01.463 --> 00:09:04.453
which is oh seven and oh
nine: the owner trap.

140
00:09:04.453 --> 00:09:09.896
And its member policy activates without
approval, for eight hours instead of four,

141
00:09:09.896 --> 00:09:15.019
and with M F A instead of the authentication
context c1: four oh eight findings,

142
00:09:15.019 --> 00:09:19.502
for the duration, the enablement
rules, the context and the approval.

143
00:09:19.502 --> 00:09:23.297
On the Azure owner group, P R
V T1 Azure Owner Sub Workload:

144
00:09:23.297 --> 00:09:28.577
it isn't role-assignable, which is a warning
for a group that holds no Entra role,

145
00:09:28.577 --> 00:09:33.527
and it has a nested group, the legacy
help desk group, as an eligible member.

146
00:09:33.527 --> 00:09:37.930
That's oh five, oh nine, and
an oh eight for a policy rule.

147
00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:44.943
Step six reads the diff,
one line per change.

148
00:09:44.943 --> 00:09:49.479
A tilde is a change, a plus an
addition, a minus a removal.

149
00:09:49.479 --> 00:09:51.393
The policy rules come first.

150
00:09:51.393 --> 00:09:55.722
The Tier 0 group's member activation
goes from eight hours to four;

151
00:09:55.722 --> 00:10:00.454
M F A leaves its enablement rules,
which keep justification and a ticket;

152
00:10:00.454 --> 00:10:03.581
the authentication context
goes from off to c1;

153
00:10:03.581 --> 00:10:07.992
and it goes from no approval to
approval, by the same approver group.

154
00:10:07.992 --> 00:10:11.681
The Azure owner group's eligible
owners must now have an end.

155
00:10:11.681 --> 00:10:12.817
Then the grants.

156
00:10:12.817 --> 00:10:18.317
An Exchange admin member's end date
moves out to April twenty twenty-seven,

157
00:10:18.317 --> 00:10:22.373
on change C H G ten oh two, and
a new active member is added,

158
00:10:22.373 --> 00:10:25.168
on C H G ten oh five,
each with its ticket.

159
00:10:25.168 --> 00:10:29.739
Then the three removals: the Tier
0 group's ungranted active member,

160
00:10:29.739 --> 00:10:32.980
its eligible owner, and
the nested legacy group.

161
00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:36.734
Last, the plan names exactly the
write permissions it would need:

162
00:10:36.734 --> 00:10:39.940
the three PIM for Groups
ReadWrite permissions.

163
00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:45.902
Step seven shows a guard refusing.

164
00:10:45.902 --> 00:10:49.791
Run the same plan with Apply,
but with Max Removals set to two.

165
00:10:49.791 --> 00:10:53.551
Guard G six refuses: three
removals is more than two;

166
00:10:53.551 --> 00:10:56.865
review them, and run
again with a higher limit.

167
00:10:56.865 --> 00:10:58.040
Nothing is sent.

168
00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:01.078
Step eight applies to the
fake with the default limit.

169
00:11:01.078 --> 00:11:06.496
The first command stops before any write:
the plan sets an authentication context,

170
00:11:06.496 --> 00:11:09.604
so confirm its Conditional
Access policy is On,

171
00:11:09.604 --> 00:11:12.791
from Module 3, then pass
Activation Gate Checked.

172
00:11:12.791 --> 00:11:17.986
The second has the same findings and the
same ten changes, the guards all pass,

173
00:11:17.986 --> 00:11:20.543
and ten requests are sent, none failed:

174
00:11:20.543 --> 00:11:24.090
the policy rules first, then
the grants and removals.

175
00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:27.478
The fake records the requests
and doesn't change its tenant.

176
00:11:27.478 --> 00:11:30.855
Step nine runs the tests,
three hundred thirty pass,

177
00:11:30.855 --> 00:11:33.380
and you delete the output folder.

178
00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:41.984
Lab 2 runs in a worldwide or G C
C test tenant with Microsoft Entra

179
00:11:41.984 --> 00:11:47.205
I D P2 or Governance licenses and
fictional people, never production.

180
00:11:47.205 --> 00:11:51.530
Use groups made for the lab, because a
group can't be taken back out of PIM.

181
00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:55.330
As a Privileged Role Administrator,
make the lab groups by hand.

182
00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:57.817
Register a reader app and a writer app.

183
00:11:57.817 --> 00:12:01.548
Plan as the reader, apply the
reviewed diff as the writer,

184
00:12:01.548 --> 00:12:04.468
then run the plan again
and see zero changes.

185
00:12:04.468 --> 00:12:07.889
The reader gets the four read
permissions from step three;

186
00:12:07.889 --> 00:12:12.167
the writer gets those and the three
writes from step six, and nothing else.

187
00:12:12.167 --> 00:12:15.674
To back out, empty both lists
in your file and apply it,

188
00:12:15.674 --> 00:12:19.017
so no schedule remains,
then delete the lab folder.

189
00:12:19.017 --> 00:12:21.520
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

190
00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:26.657
The federal requirement.

191
00:12:26.657 --> 00:12:28.061
The P R V groups' file,

192
00:12:28.061 --> 00:12:32.445
the check and the plan are the
evidence for the plan's task A2.1.

193
00:12:32.445 --> 00:12:37.778
From NIST special publication eight
hundred fifty-three they support A C six,

194
00:12:37.778 --> 00:12:43.278
enhancement five, privileged accounts
restricted to defined roles; A C six and A

195
00:12:43.278 --> 00:12:48.695
C five, least privilege and separation of
duties; and A C two, account management.

196
00:12:48.695 --> 00:12:53.637
And from CISA's B O D twenty-five
oh-one and its ScubaGear baseline:

197
00:12:53.637 --> 00:12:57.847
M S A A D seven point four, no
permanent active assignment,

198
00:12:57.847 --> 00:13:01.783
and seven point five,
provisioning only through PIM.

199
00:13:01.783 --> 00:13:03.645
C M three covers the change:

200
00:13:03.645 --> 00:13:07.457
reviewed before it's made,
and an unsafe change stopped.

201
00:13:07.457 --> 00:13:13.272
The evidence an ISSO keeps: the reviewed
file, with each holder's end date and

202
00:13:13.272 --> 00:13:19.740
ticket; each plan's diff, plan and findings;
and each apply's results and manifest.

203
00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:25.465
Check yourself with two questions.

204
00:13:25.465 --> 00:13:30.865
First: the file lists three removals and
you pass Max Removals two with Apply.

205
00:13:30.865 --> 00:13:31.877
What happens?

206
00:13:31.877 --> 00:13:36.828
The answer is b: guard G six refuses
the plan, and nothing is sent.

207
00:13:36.828 --> 00:13:39.115
You saw it in Lab 1, step seven.

208
00:13:39.115 --> 00:13:43.515
Second: why does the pipeline's
app not create the P R V groups?

209
00:13:43.515 --> 00:13:46.569
The answer is b: creating
a role-assignable group

210
00:13:46.569 --> 00:13:49.853
needs Role Management dot
ReadWrite dot Directory.

211
00:13:49.853 --> 00:13:52.692
The program keeps that
permission off the pipeline,

212
00:13:52.692 --> 00:13:56.191
and a Privileged Role Administrator
creates the groups by hand.

213
00:13:56.191 --> 00:14:01.440
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

214
00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:06.015
To sum up.

215
00:14:06.015 --> 00:14:10.696
You read a fictional tenant's P R V
groups against a reviewed file of four,

216
00:14:10.696 --> 00:14:15.615
and found a standing member nobody granted,
an owner trap, and a nested group.

217
00:14:15.615 --> 00:14:18.717
You read the exact requests
that would fix them,

218
00:14:18.717 --> 00:14:23.806
saw a guard refuse a plan with too many
removals, saw the apply refuse to select

219
00:14:23.806 --> 00:14:28.578
c1 until a person confirmed the gate,
and applied ten requests to the fake.

220
